<quiz><question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating the probability mass function for different values of x</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<style>
table, th, td {
  border:1px solid black; text-align:center;
}
</style>

<br>

<p>Six lots of components are ready to be shipped by a supplier. The number of defective components in each lot is as follows:</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Lots</th>
    <th>Lot 1</th>
    <th>Lot 2</th>
    <th>Lot 3</th>
    <th>Lot 4</th>
    <th>Lot 5</th>
    <th>Lot 6</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Number of defectives</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[1]@}</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[2]@}</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[3]@}</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[4]@}</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[5]@}</td>
    <td>{@num_def_list[6]@}</td>
 </tr>
</table>


<br>

<p>Determine the probability distribution of \(X\) by filling the table below.</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>\(X\)</th>
    <th>0</th>
    <th>1</th>
    <th>2</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>\(f(x) = p(X=x)=\)</td>
    <td>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</td>
    <td>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</td>
    <td>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</td>
 </tr>
</table>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>worked out solution</b></p>
<p>Let \(X\) be the number of defectives in the selected lot.The three possible \(X\) values are \(0, \, 1\) and \(2\). Of the six likely samples events, three results in  \(X=0\) one in  \(X=1\)  and the other two in \(X=2\). Let \(f(x)=p(X=x)\) then \(f(0)={@ta1@}, f(1)={@ta2@}\) and \(f(2)={@ta3@}\). The value of \(X\) along with their probabilities collectively specify the probability distribution or probability mass function of \(X\).
The probability mass function can be specified in a table.</p>
<p>The probability mass function can be specified in a table</p>

<style>
table, th, td {
  border:1px solid black; text-align:center;
}
</style>
<body>

<table style="width:100%">
  <tr>
    <td>\(X\)</td>
    <td>{@0@}</td>
    <td>{@1@}</td>
    <td>{@2@}</td>
  </tr>
    <tr>
    <td>\(f(x)=p(X=x)\)</td>
    <td>{@ta1@}</td>
    <td>{@ta2@}</td>
    <td>{@ta3@}</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<br>
<p>This means that if this experiment was repeated over and over again, in the long run \(X=0\) would occur {@ta1@} of the time, \(X=1\) will occur {@ta2@} of the time and \(X=2\) will occur {@ta3@} of the time .</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
